  "narration": {
    "title": "The Score Puts Numbers to the Award Chatter",
    "intro": "Today we run the MVP, Cy Young and Rookie races the way our 0-100 Score ranks them. Two of the six leaders are runaways; the other four are dogfights where a single hot month could flip the order. Here's who's in front, and who's breathing down their neck.",
    "mvp_lead": "In the AL, Yordan Alvarez tops the board at an 84.7 Score behind a .317 average and a 1.040 OPS, well clear of Ben Rice (77.3, 34 homers) and Junior Caminero (76.8). The NL is the real fight: James Wood leads at 78.5, but Shohei Ohtani (77.9) and Matt Olson (77.7) are right on top of him, and Ohtani has been the hottest of the three lately with a .981 OPS over his last 30 days.",
    "cy_lead": "Jacob Misiorowski is the closest thing to a lock anywhere in these races, an 89.8 Score fueled by a 1.75 ERA, a 0.75 WHIP and 210 strikeouts, nearly nine points ahead of Chris Sale (80.9) in the NL. The AL is tighter at the top, where Cam Schlittler (81.3, 189 K) leads Dylan Cease (72.5) even though Cease owns more strikeouts and a scalding 1.62 ERA over his last month.",
    "roy_lead": "Munetaka Murakami paces the AL rookies at a 74.3 Score, his .904 OPS and 29 homers giving the White Sox a first baseman after years of sub-.750 production at the spot, with Parker Messick (69.7, 2.54 ERA) next in line. In the NL, Sal Stewart holds the top spot at 71.1 on the back of 94 RBI and a red-hot last month, ahead of Nolan McLean and TJ Rumfield."
  }
